NOTES = {
1: "This deck covers chapter 12 of Mastering Bitcoin, third edition, on mining and consensus. It explains how a miner earns a reward, how every node checks blocks and chooses between competing chains, how proof of work sets the pace of block production, and how the rules of Bitcoin are changed by forks. Every number on a result picture was computed with the chapter's own Python library or read from one recorded run of Bitcoin Core 31.1 on private test chains. The course page for chapter 12 holds the same examples and lets you edit and run them.",
2: "The route has six parts that follow the order of the chapter: money and rewards, verifying and agreeing, building the block, proof of work, competition and attacks, and changing the rules. Four true stories and twenty-two code examples are spread across them. The examples appear in the same order under Run slide code on the course page, so every listing in this deck can be run and edited there. Use the slide numbers in the list to jump to a part, and use this slide to see how much of the chapter each part carries.",
3: "These seven questions are the chapter's own, rewritten so that each one has an answer on a named slide. The first asks how a reward is made and why the supply is capped, the second which checks a transaction and a block must pass, the third why the chain with the most work wins, the fourth what a candidate block holds, the fifth how target, difficulty and retargeting set the pace, the sixth why small miners join pools, and the seventh how forks change the rules. The summary at the end returns to them, so you can test yourself.",
4: "The chapter has six parts, and together they hold the key concepts that the research found in the book: six for money and rewards, four for verifying and agreeing, nine for building the block, ten for proof of work, eleven for competition and attacks and eleven for changing the rules. Each part opens with a divider, continues with concept cards and code that runs, and some parts include a true story. Use this map to place any later slide in its part, and to see which parts carry the most examples.",
5: "A miner is paid in new coins and in fees. This part shows how the subsidy of new coins halves on a fixed schedule, why the total supply stops just below 21 million coins, and how Bitcoin Core checks that a miner claims no more than the subsidy plus the fees of the block. The two boxes name the ideas: the subsidy schedule, which example 1 reads at five heights, and the rule that a block pays subsidy plus fees and no more, which example 3 checks on a private chain.",
6: "These cards cover the six key concepts of the part on money and rewards. The reward is cut in half after every 210,000 blocks, about every four years. All rewards ever paid add up to 2,099,999,997,690,000 satoshis, just short of 21 million coins, because each halving is a right shift of whole satoshis. The book's printed script divides with a slash and so ends in a float. The reward is subsidy plus fees; block 840,000 paid fees twelve times its subsidy. Regtest halves every 150 blocks. The course page explains each term in full.",
7: "The subsidy is computed from the height of a block. Five heights, one at the start of each period of 210,000 blocks, give rewards of 50, 25, 12.5, 6.25 and 3.125 coins. The halving is a right shift of whole satoshis, so after the 33rd halving a single satoshi is left, and after the 34th the subsidy is zero. Summing every reward in whole satoshis sets the total supply, which the next example computes. This is example 1 under Run slide code on the course page; change a height there and run it again.",
8: "Adding the 33 non-zero rewards in whole satoshis gives 2,099,999,997,690,000, which is 2,310,000 satoshis below 21 million coins. The book's printed script divides with a slash, which in Python 3 is a float division, so run as printed it ends at a float and never shows the shortfall. The lesson is that money must be counted in integers, as Bitcoin Core does. Compare this with the right shift on the previous slide, which is the integer form of the same halving. This is example 2 on the course page.",
9: "On a private chain, Bitcoin Core offers a block template that states the reward. The reward equals the subsidy plus the fees of the chosen transactions: 305,175 plus 40,194 satoshis, or 345,369 in all. A miner may claim less than that, and a block that claims one satoshi less is accepted. A block that claims one satoshi more is refused with the reason bad-cb-amount. The reward is therefore an upper limit that every node enforces. This is example 3 on the course page; the story of block 840,000 on the next slide shows how large fees can be.",
10: "This true story is dated 20 April 2024. Block 840,000, mined by ViaBTC at 00:09:27 UTC, was the first to pay the new subsidy of 3.125 coins, half of 6.25. Its 3,050 transactions paid 3,762,561,499 satoshis in fees, 12.04 times the subsidy, so the reward was mostly fees. The sum was recomputed from the saved block, and its coinbase carries the tag ViaBTC, which pool statistics use. The book says that fees are a small part of a miner's income; this block is an exception. The picture is a stand-in, and the link opens the block on mempool.space.",
11: "No one is in charge of Bitcoin, so every node checks everything itself. This part runs the rules for transactions and blocks on a private Bitcoin Core node, and then shows why nodes follow the chain with the most accumulated work and not the chain with the most blocks. The two boxes name the ideas: independent checking, which examples 4 and 5 demonstrate with named reject reasons, and choosing the chain, which example 6 demonstrates with two competing branches. Each refusal carries a reason that names the broken rule.",
12: "These cards cover the four key concepts of verifying and agreeing. Each transaction is tested against fixed rules and refused with a named reason. Each block must also obey the rules of reward, height, merkle root, time and version. A mining reward can be spent only after 100 confirmations, which is called coinbase maturity. Nodes follow the chain with the most accumulated work, not the one with the most blocks. The first three concepts appear in examples 4 and 5, and the fourth in example 6. The course page explains each term in full.",
13: "Each node tests every transaction against the same rules. A private Bitcoin Core node was asked about spoiled transactions, and each broken rule came back with a name: a duplicate input, a negative output and a coinbase spent too early, three reasons in all. The coinbase output is refused at depth 99 and allowed at depth 100, which is the maturity rule. A lock time in the future is refused as non-final. These reasons are the strings Core returns, so they are the same names you will meet in logs. This is example 4 on the course page.",
14: "The block checks go further than the transaction checks. A private Bitcoin Core node was given fourteen test blocks that each broke exactly one rule, and one valid block. The valid block was accepted. A reward one satoshi too high was refused as bad-cb-amount, a missing height as bad-cb-height, a wrong merkle root as bad-txnmrklroot, a time equal to the median time past as time-too-old, a time two hours and a second ahead as time-too-new, and an old version as bad-version. This is example 5 on the course page.",
15: "Two branches were built on a private chain, and the first had paid Carol. The second branch carried more chain work, 208 against 206, so Core switched to it, and the payment fell out of the chain: Carol held 49.999 coins before and nothing after, and the payment went from one confirmation to minus two. A node therefore follows the chain with the most work, not the longest chain. This is why a shop waits for confirmations before it hands over goods. This is example 6 on the course page.",
16: "Before the search for a hash starts, the miner builds a candidate block. This part reads the coinbase that Bitcoin Core makes today, takes an 80-byte header apart, and shows how the extra nonce opens a new search space. The three boxes name the pieces: the block template, which a node fills with transactions by fee rate up to a weight limit; the coinbase, the first transaction, which creates the reward and carries the height and a witness commitment; and the header, with six fields in 80 bytes. Examples 7, 8 and 9 cover them.",
17: "These cards cover the first six concepts of building the block. A node offers a template with transactions picked by fee rate, up to a weight limit. Core 31.1 fills in the lock time, the sequence and the end of the coinbase script in ways the book's table does not show. The free bytes of the coinbase script are where pools sign their blocks. The BIP 34 height push makes every coinbase different. The witness commitment is an extra coinbase output. The extra nonce space gives 2 to the 96 headers to try.",
18: "The block template of Bitcoin Core has a weight limit of 4,000,000 units, which leaves room for the header. The coinbase script starts with the block height as a short pushed number: height 2204 becomes the bytes 029c08, and Core adds a zero byte. Core sets other fields itself and not as the book lists them: the lock time is the height minus one, the sequence is 0xfffffffe, and the version is 2. This is example 7 on the course page; the BIP 34 page of the BIPs describes the height rule.",
19: "These cards cover the remaining concepts of building the block. When a miner has used up the nonce, a counter in the coinbase script changes the merkle root and so opens a fresh search space. A block's time must be later than the median of the last eleven blocks, which is called median time past, and a block time more than two hours ahead of a node's clock is refused. The extra nonce appears in example 9, and the two time rules were tested in example 5. The course page explains each term in full.",
20: "The header of a regtest block is eighty bytes with six fields: version, previous block hash, merkle root, time, bits and nonce. Its hash, read as a number, is not larger than the target that the bits describe. On regtest the bits are 207fffff, a huge target, so six leading zero bits are more than enough and the nonce can stay at zero. On the main chain the target is far smaller, which is why real mining needs vast numbers of tries. This is example 8 on the course page.",
21: "A miner that has used up the 32-bit nonce changes a counter in the coinbase script. That changes the coinbase transaction id, the merkle root and so the whole header, while the header's own nonce can start again from zero. In the recorded run, two builds of the same block differ only in the extra nonce: the coinbase ids differ, the merkle roots differ and the header hashes differ, while the header nonces are equal. This is example 9 on the course page, and it connects to the nonce space on the next part.",
22: "Proof of work is a search that is costly to run and cheap to check. This part plays the search with a text, reads the target from the bits, turns difficulty into days of waiting, and follows the retarget rule on a real period of 2024. The three boxes name the ideas: the nonce search, in which numbers are tried until the hash falls below the target and checking takes one hash; target and difficulty, held in a four-byte code; and retargeting every 2,016 blocks so that blocks keep coming every ten minutes.",
23: "These cards cover the first six concepts of proof of work. The Hello World nonce hashes a text with a counter added until the hash starts with zeros. Finding a valid header takes billions of hashes and checking it takes one. A header is valid when its hash, read as a number, is not above the target. The target is stored in four bytes called compact bits. A miner doing one terahash a second waited about 59 days at the difficulty of block 277,316. Difficulty times 2 to the 32, over 600 seconds, estimates the network hash rate.",
24: "The book hashes the text Hello, world! with a counter added. The first hash that starts with a zero comes at counter 32. Every further zero digit makes the search about sixteen times longer: the tries needed for one, two, three and four zero digits are 32, 228, 3,460 and 27,835. Checking the answer always takes a single hash. The book prints the hash 09cb91f8 for a capital W, but that is the hash of the lower-case text; the capital W gives 6bbe6a3d. This is example 10 on the course page.",
25: "This true story is dated 1 August 2002. Proof of work did not start with Bitcoin. Cynthia Dwork and Moni Naor proposed in 1992 that a user should compute a moderately hard function before using a shared resource, to fight junk mail. Adam Back proposed Hashcash in 1997 and wrote it up in a paper dated 1 August 2002. The sender hashes a string with a growing counter until the hash starts with zeros, and the receiver checks it with one hash. Nakamoto's 2008 paper calls Bitcoin's proof of work similar to Hashcash. The lesson is that a cheap check of an expensive search can price any request.",
26: "These cards cover the last four concepts of proof of work. The 2015-gap retarget notes that 2,016 blocks have only 2,015 gaps, and the rule measures the span from the first block to the last. The factor-of-four clamp means that one retarget never moves the target by more than a factor of four either way. The BIP 94 branch lets testnet4 measure the period from its first block. On regtest a chain parameter switches retargeting off. Examples 13, 14 and 15 compute each of these.",
27: "The header stores the target in four bytes called bits: one byte for the size and three for the digits. Block 277,316 has the bits 1903a30c, which describe a target of 194 bits. Difficulty compares this target with the easiest target that Bitcoin allows, and here it is 1,180,923,195. A smaller target means more zero bits at the start of a valid hash: this block needed 62 of them. The bits can be rebuilt from the target, so the encoding loses nothing. This is example 11 on the course page.",
28: "At a given difficulty a block takes about difficulty times 2 to the 32 hashes. A miner doing one terahash a second waits 58.7 days, or 8,453 blocks' worth of time, at the difficulty of block 277,316; the book says 8,496 blocks, and the difference comes from the exact figure used. The same formula applied to a later difficulty, 132,716,002,350,731.3 typed in, estimates the network hash rate at about 9.5e20 hashes a second. This is example 12 on the course page.",
29: "Every 2,016 blocks Bitcoin scales the target by the real duration of the period over the intended fourteen days. The period before block 840,672 took 1,186,047 seconds, a little under fourteen days, so the target shrank by about 2.0 percent and mining became a little harder. The rule gives the bits 0x170331db, which are the bits the chain really has. Counting 2,016 blocks instead of 2,015 gaps would add a small bias of 0.05 percent. This is example 13 on the course page.",
30: "A period of 2,016 blocks may be much faster or slower than planned. To protect the network, the rule limits the change of the target to a quarter or four times. For a period of 100,000 seconds the ratio of new target to old target stops at 0.25; for exactly 1,209,600 seconds, fourteen days, it is 1.0; and for 10 million seconds it stops at 4.0. Difficulty moves by the inverse, so it also changes by at most a factor of four. This is example 14 on the course page.",
31: "Each chain has its own constants in the source file chainparams.cpp. Regtest sets a flag that stops all retargeting, so its bits stay 207fffff at block 2017. Testnet4 enforces BIP 94, a branch of the retarget code that measures the period from its first block and that the book's listing does not have. Mainnet has neither flag. Reading the flags from the saved source shows what a chain does, and Core's pow.cpp has the branch for BIP 94. This is example 15 on the course page.",
32: "Mining is a race between miners, and a large share of the power can attack. The nonce space runs out, so miners vary other header fields; small miners form pools and share the pay at the price of trust; and a share of the power buys the odds of rewriting recent history. The three boxes name the ideas, and examples 16, 17 and 18 compute each of them: how often the nonce space is emptied, how long a small miner waits for a block, and the odds of an attacker catching up.",
33: "These cards cover the first six concepts of competition and attacks. 32 bits give 4.3 billion tries, which a modern machine uses up in a fraction of a second. Version rolling lets 16 bits of the version field change freely. Covert ASICBoost builds the header so that hardware does less work per hash. A pool pays members for easy partial solutions and keeps a fee. Stratum protocols are the language of pools and mining machines. A solo miner may wait years for one block, so income is very uneven.",
34: "The network hash rate of 15 October 2026, typed from the saved figure, is 1.029e21 hashes a second. A machine could empty the 32-bit nonce about 2.4e11 times every second at that rate, so the nonce alone is far too small a search space. Version rolling adds 16 free bits to the version field, and the extra nonce in the coinbase adds more. The comparison of 32 bits with 16 bits shows how much the two tricks add. This is example 16 on the course page.",
35: "These cards cover the next three concepts of competition and attacks. Block withholding is a pool member who hides a full solution and so harms the pool and not themselves. Nakamoto's catch-up odds fall quickly with the number of blocks behind. The selfish-mining threshold is the share above which hiding blocks can pay; it lies between one quarter and one third of the power. Example 18 computes both numbers, and the course page explains each term in full.",
36: "The network finds 144 blocks a day. A miner with a share s of the power expects 144 times s blocks a day, so the wait for one block is the inverse of that, here counted in days: 0.1 days for a tenth of the power, 0.7 days for one percent, 6.9 days for a tenth of a percent and 69.4 days for a hundredth of a percent. A pool pays out small regular shares instead of one rare block, which smooths the income at the price of trust. This is example 17 on the course page.",
37: "These cards cover the last two concepts of competition and attacks. The largest pool of the last year found about 28 percent of the blocks, which is between the thresholds of one quarter and one third, so it is close to the share at which selfish mining pays. A majority of the power can rewrite recent history and refuse transactions, but it cannot forge signatures or create coins that the rules do not allow. The numbers come from example 18 and from the saved pool statistics.",
38: "Nakamoto's formula gives the chance that an attacker with a share q ever catches up from six blocks behind: about 0.0002 for 10 percent, 0.1321 for 30 percent and 0.7661 for 45 percent. Selfish mining pays above a share that depends on how many honest miners follow the selfish block: one third if none follow, one quarter if half follow, and zero if all follow. The thresholds fall as more honest miners follow. This is example 18 on the course page.",
39: "The rules of Bitcoin change by forks. Looser rules split the chain into two histories, which is called a hard fork. Stricter rules stay valid for old nodes and are switched on by a vote, which is called a soft fork. This part lets a looser and a stricter node disagree on a private chain, times a minority chain, follows a BIP 9 vote through its states, and reads the heights of old soft forks in the source code. Examples 19 to 22 do this.",
40: "These cards cover the first six concepts of changing the rules. A soft fork only tightens the rules, so old nodes still accept the new blocks. A minority chain keeps the old difficulty and runs slowly. In March 2013 a large block was valid for new nodes and refused by old ones, and the chain split for hours. The Bitcoin Cash fork of 2017 produced a second coin. BIP 34 was the first soft fork switched on by counting block versions. The BIP 9 state machine moves a deployment from defined through started and locked in to active.",
41: "A node with the old, looser rule and a node with the new, stricter rule saw the same block. The loose node accepted a block that has no height, and the strict node refused it as bad-cb-height. The loose node went on building, and the strict node followed only blocks that obey the new rule, so the two chains ended with different tips, at heights 6 and 3. This is the difference between a rule that was loosened and one that was tightened. This is example 19 on the course page.",
42: "A hard fork loosens the rules. New blocks may break the old rules, so old nodes refuse them and the chain splits into two histories. A soft fork tightens the rules. New blocks still obey the old rules, so old nodes accept them and the chain stays whole. A soft fork therefore needs most of the miners to enforce it, but not all nodes, while a hard fork needs every node to upgrade. Example 19 showed both nodes reacting to the same block.",
43: "These cards cover the last five concepts of changing the rules. BIP 8 counts in block heights, not time, and can force the lock-in when the timeout comes. Segwit was held back under BIP 9 until BIP 91 and BIP 148, and became active on 24 August 2017. Speedy Trial was a three-month vote that needed 90 percent, followed by a delay before the new rules began. Old soft forks are now plain activation heights in the source code, which are called buried deployments. BIP 54 proposes to fix the time warp attack and to limit signature operations and transaction size.",
44: "After a split, a branch keeps the old difficulty but has only a share of the hash rate, so blocks come more slowly and the next retarget is far away. With 80 percent of the power the 2,016 blocks take 18 days, with half of it 28 days, with a fifth 70 days and with a tenth 140 days instead of 14. In March 2013 the old chain was kept for only six hours for this reason. This is example 20 on the course page.",
45: "This true story is dated 11 March 2013. On that day at 23:24:19 UTC block 225,430, with 797 transactions, was valid for Bitcoin 0.8 and refused by some older nodes, so the chain split for 24 blocks, about six hours. Older versions had too few Berkeley DB locks for a large block, and this accident had become a rule of consensus. Marek Palatinus and Michael Marsee moved their pools back to 0.7 so that the old chain carried the most work, and Gavin Andresen wrote BIP 50. The lesson is that an accident in software can become a rule of the network.",
46: "A deployment is defined, started, locked in and then active. Bitcoin Core, on a private chain, reported the state of a test deployment at six heights, and the last state was active. BIP 9 asked for 95 percent of 2,016 blocks, which is 1,916; the Speedy Trial of taproot asked for 90 percent, which is 1,815. The version 0x20200004 signals bit 2, which is the signal that block 709,632 carried. This is example 21 on the course page.",
47: "This true story is dated 14 November 2021. Taproot was activated by a variant of BIP 9 called Speedy Trial, which opened on 24 April 2021, timed out on 11 August 2021 and needed 1,815 of 2,016 blocks, 90 percent, instead of 1,916. A short vote makes a soft fork succeed or fail quickly, and a minimum activation height gave nodes time to upgrade. F2Pool mined block 709,632 with version 0x20200004, in which bit 2 is the taproot signal. The lesson is that fail fast or succeed: a short vote keeps a soft fork calm.",
48: "Once a soft fork is old and settled, Bitcoin Core writes its activation as a plain height in the source file chainparams.cpp. The heights of four soft forks on the main chain were read from the saved file: BIP 34 at 227,931, BIP 66 at 363,725, CSV at 419,328 and Segwit at 481,824. A buried fork is no longer voted on; it is simply part of the rules. This is example 22 on the course page.",
49: "These five lines are the deck in miniature, and each can be proved from its slides. The reward is subsidy plus fees. Every node checks by the same rules and follows the most work. A candidate block has a template, a coinbase and a header whose nonce, extra nonce and version are searched. Proof of work is a hash below a target, kept at ten minutes by retargeting. Pools smooth income, attacks need a large share, and soft forks tighten the rules by a vote. A line that feels unproven sends you to the code slide of its part.",
50: "Everything on this slide is free to read or watch, and every link was opened on the date in the subtitle. The reading list has the Optech topics on soft fork activation and on selfish mining, the block explorer mempool.space, BIP 9, BIP 54 and the paper of Eyal and Sirer. The videos are from 3Blue1Brown and Khan Academy. The communities are the Bitcoin Optech newsletters, the research forum Delving Bitcoin and the bitcoin-dev mailing list. A good start is the 3Blue1Brown video, then the Optech topic on soft forks next to the chapter.",
51: "These four claims are what you should now be able to say, and each one is carried by a computation that ran on a slide of this deck: how the reward is fixed, how nodes check and choose a chain, how proof of work sets the pace, and how pools, attacks and forks matter. The course page for chapter 12 holds every word behind them, the stories with their sources and the question bank, so you can test yourself and follow the links further.",
}

